{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>For from the head of the rocks I see him.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): this is the high place mentioned above, וילך שפי." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: צורים are the patriarchs, and מגבעות are the tribes. And he called the patriarchs צורים because they are likened to mountains, as it is said (Micah 6:2): ״שמעו הרים את ריב ה׳״, and it is written (ibid.): ״קום ריב את ההרים״; and since Abraham was the head of them and the head of faith and unity, therefore Scripture called him ראש צורים. And he called the twelve tribes of Jacob גבעות because they are lower than the rank of the patriarchs, just as the hills are lower than the great rocks. And after he mentioned the merit of their beginning — the mountains, which were the foundation of their lineage and the root of their descent, being the good root from which the merit of the branches would be proclaimed — he proceeded to explain the merit of their end, which is what he said: הן עם לבדד ישכון ובגוים לא יתחשב. And the verses connect thus: he said, how can I curse and how can I rage, when I see this nation whose foundation and root is great, and whose uprooting is no light thing — neither from the side of their beginning nor from the side of their end: not from the side of their beginning, because their root is strong with the strength of the rock, and there is no power in man to uproot it, for they trace and descend from the צורים and גבעות, which are the patriarchs and the tribes, whose merit is strong as rock; and therefore it is impossible for me to come against them, neither from the side of their beginning nor from the side of their end, for they are destined to inherit the world at the end of days, and all the gentile nations will perish while they alone endure — and this is what he said: לבדד ישכון. And the word בדד means 'alone,' in the manner of what is said (Lamentations 1:1): ״איכה ישבה בדד״; and the singular is called בדד because the singular one isolates himself, and the word לבדד includes both בדד and לבדם, for the term serves two ways; and the intention when he said לבדד is that it is a nation singled out, not reckoned among the other nations, but separated from them in its Torah and its faith, in the manner of what is written (Leviticus 20:26): ״ואבדיל אתכם מן העמים להיות לי״; and when he said לבדד the intention is that they are a nation singled out לבדד, that is, for the Name that is singled out, concerning which it is written (Deuteronomy 32:12): ״ה׳ בדד ינחנו״; and because of this he used לבדד, for it would have been fitting to say הן עם בדד ישכון, in the manner that Moses said (ibid. 33:28): ״וישכן ישראל בטח בדד״; but the meaning alludes that the people is singled out among the other nations and not reckoned, and it is singled out for the Name that is singled out, which is the Elohim of Israel, who among the gods of the nations is not reckoned." "<b>And behold,</b> Moses our master, peace be upon him, alluded in his words when he said (ibid. 33:29): ״אשריך ישראל מי כמוך״, and when he said (ibid. 26): ״אין כאל ישורון״ — explaining that Israel is a singled-out people, there is none like them among the nations, and likewise the Elohim of Israel is singled out, there is none like Him among the gods of the nations. And so we find that Daniel prophesied concerning the perishing of all the gentile nations and that the kingdom would endure for Israel forever — this is what he mentioned concerning the perishing of the gentile nations (Daniel 7:11): ״והובד גשמה ויהיבת ליקידת אשא״, and concerning the perishing of the rest of the nations (ibid. 12): ״ושאר חיותא העדיו שלטנהון וארכא בחיין יהיבת להון עד זמן ועדן״; and concerning the everlasting endurance of Israel he said afterward, concerning King Messiah (ibid. 13–14): ״וארו עם ענני שמיא כבר אנש אתה הוא ועד עתיק יומיא מטא וקדמוהי הקרבוהי, וליה יהיב שולטן ויקר ומלכו וכל עממיא אומיא ולשניא ליה יפלחון שלטניה שלטן עלם די לא יעדה ומלכותיה די לא תתחבל״." "<b>And by way of</b> reason (sekhel): צורים are the seven wandering stars (planets), גבעות are the twelve constellations. And he likened the stars, which are fixed in the sphere, to the rocks fixed in the earth, for just as building stones for the dwellers of clay houses are hewn from rocks, so too the structures of this lower world and the actions within it are hewn and proceed from the stars. And he likened the sphere to the head, which is the beginning of the body and its uppermost part — so too the sphere is the beginning of causes and the origin of actions, through the power and decree of the Creator. And this man the soothsayer had no portion in the Elohim of Israel, but he had a portion in the stars and constellations; and through his great wisdom in the science of astronomy he would see, in the judgments of the stars and the wisdom of the constellations, the great destiny to which Israel is destined in the future — that they shall dwell לבדד, not intermingling with the nations and not exchanging the glory of their faith for another faith; and this is what he said: הן עם לבדד ישכון ובגוים לא יתחשב."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:35:38.490211396Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}